  "tree_id": 1097,
  "family": "Pittosporaceae",
  "memo": "&lt;em&gt;Pittosporum undulatum &lt;/em&gt;is a large shrub to tree with pale gray branches and an inviting, heavy shade. Its small white flowers are strongly fragrant at night, attracting moths for pollination. Once mature, its orange fruit capsules open and drop sticky seeds enjoyed by birds. This Australian-native is resistant to drought and honey fungus. With its high seed production rate and popularity as an ornamental, it has become invasive across regions like California, South Africa, and Hawai'i. This species is considered a weed in some parts of its natural habitat due to suppressed fire regimes.&nbsp;",
